---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";

const curve = [
  { ms: 50, wins: 38 },
  { ms: 100, wins: 12 },
  { ms: 150, wins: 2 },
  { ms: 200, wins: 0 },
  { ms: 250, wins: 0 },
  { ms: 300, wins: 0 },
];
const RUNS = 51;
const BAR_W = 56;
const GAP = 24;
const CHART_H = 160;
const chartWidth = curve.length * (BAR_W + GAP) - GAP;

const blocks = [
  { weeks: "1–2", name: "The instrument", href: "/lectures/week-01/", line: "Turn an opinion into a prediction, and make the game run with no screen." },
  { weeks: "3–5", name: "Players and distributions", href: "/lectures/week-03/", line: "A player with a reaction time, 51 runs at a time, and dials that do not respond evenly." },
  { weeks: "6–8", name: "Options and gates", href: "/lectures/week-06/", line: "What each choice is worth, how big a wall has to be, and who wins by not playing properly." },
  { weeks: "9–10", name: "The edges of measurement", href: "/lectures/week-09/", line: "How the instrument lies without going red. Then you finally play the game." },
  { weeks: "11–12", name: "Different players", href: "/lectures/week-11/", line: "The same boss stops 1 player in 51 or 41 in 51. Pick a number and defend it." },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p class="lede">
    “The boss is too hard” is not an opinion. It is a prediction nobody has
    written down yet.
  </p>

  <figure class="curve">
    <svg
      viewBox={`0 0 ${chartWidth} ${CHART_H + 44}`}
      role="img"
      aria-label={`Wins out of ${RUNS} runs for a simulated player at six reaction times: ${curve.map((p) => `${p.ms} milliseconds, ${p.wins} wins`).join("; ")}.`}
    >
      {curve.map((point, i) => {
        const h = Math.max(2, (point.wins / RUNS) * CHART_H);
        const x = i * (BAR_W + GAP);
        return (
          <g>
            <rect x={x} y={CHART_H - h} width={BAR_W} height={h} rx="3" />
            <text class="value" x={x + BAR_W / 2} y={CHART_H - h - 8} text-anchor="middle">{point.wins}</text>
            <text class="label" x={x + BAR_W / 2} y={CHART_H + 24} text-anchor="middle">{point.ms} ms</text>
          </g>
        );
      })}
    </svg>
    <figcaption>
      One game, one dial, six even steps of 50 ms in a simulated player's
      reaction time. Wins out of {RUNS}. The first step costs 26 wins. The
      fourth costs none. <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}lectures/week-05/`.replace(/\/\//g, "/")}>Week 5</a> is about why.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Spend twelve weeks measuring one small game, <em>Close Quarters</em>, a
    two-minute survival run with seven upgrades and two bosses. You will pull
    its rules away from its screen, write players that stand in for people, run
    it 51 times in a sixth of a second, and learn to read what comes back as a
    distribution instead of an anecdote.
  </p>
  <p>
    You will not play it until week ten. By then you will have nine weeks of
    predictions on paper, and the game gets to surprise you in a way you can
    write down.
  </p>
  <p>
    You leave with one number: a change to one constant in that game, and a
    ten-minute case for it that three sceptical classmates could check.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    People who make or mod games and are tired of balance arguments that nobody
    can lose. You need to be able to read and edit a few hundred lines of
    TypeScript. You do not need statistics beyond a median, and you do not need
    to be good at the game.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is a narrow course on purpose: single-player games only, simulation
    only. Multiplayer balance and playtesting with real people are different
    subjects, and week ten is honest about what that leaves out.
  </p>

  <h2>The semester in five blocks</h2>
  <ol class="blocks">
    {blocks.map((block) => (
      <li>
        <span class="weeks">Weeks {block.weeks}</span>
        <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}${block.href}`.replace(/\/\//g, "/")}>{block.name}</a>
        <span class="line">{block.line}</span>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ol>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>Twelve weeks, one claim a week, every one of them about the same game.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>What you measure each Thursday, and the number you leave with.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>Four pieces that feed each other: a curve, a point on it, a lab book, a defence.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    line-height: 1.45;
    max-width: 34ch;
  }

  .curve {
    margin: 2rem 0 2.5rem;
  }

  .curve svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 30rem;
    height: auto;
    overflow: visible;
  }

  .curve rect {
    fill: var(--at-primary);
  }

  .curve .value {
    fill: var(--at-text);
    font-size: 15px;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .curve .label {
    fill: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: 13px;
  }

  .curve figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    max-width: 34rem;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .blocks {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.9rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .blocks li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 6.5rem 1fr;
    column-gap: 1rem;
  }

  .blocks .weeks {
    grid-row: span 2;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .blocks a {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .blocks .line {
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
  }

  @media (max-width: 30rem) {
    .blocks li {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .blocks .weeks {
      grid-row: auto;
    }
  }
</style>
